{"rule": "Return this JSON object with the rule field unchanged and a new passage of about 250 words in the text field.", "text": "In the spring the old clockmaker opened his shop again after the long winter, and the whole street seemed to wake with him. Tomas swept the dust from the doorstep, unlatched the shutters, and set the brass bell above the door so that it would ring whenever a customer stepped inside. The shelves were crowded with clocks of every size: tall ones in carved walnut cases, small ones with painted faces, and a single silver pocket watch that had belonged to his grandfather. Each morning he wound them one by one, listening for the moment each mechanism caught and began to tick. By noon the room was filled with a soft chorus of ticking, no two clocks exactly in step, like a crowd of people breathing. His granddaughter Elsa came after school and sat on a high stool beside the workbench. She liked to watch him open a broken clock and lay its tiny gears across a square of green felt, each piece in its proper place. He showed her how to hold the tweezers lightly, how to clean a spring without bending it, and how to listen for the faint catch of a tooth that did not quite fit. Patience, he told her, is the most important tool. One rainy afternoon he handed her the silver pocket watch, which had stopped years before. Elsa worked on it for a week, slowly and carefully. When at last it began to tick in her palm, Tomas smiled, closed her fingers gently around it, and told her it was hers to keep."}
